    "analysts": {
      "body": "Wall Street voices this week stayed fixed on Tesla\u2019s Cybercab debut and the broader AI buildout. Dan Ives used CNBC Closing Bell appearances to spotlight an intensifying U.S. AI datacenter debate, casting the tech-versus-Beltway clash as a major theme into year-end as hyperscale construction accelerates.\n\nGary Black assessed Tesla\u2019s Austin Cybercab event as largely falling short of pre-event expectations: a brief formal note that the vehicle joins the robotaxi fleet, then attendee rides in the two-seat, no-pedals cabin. He also flagged how unusual it is for a company near a $1.3 trillion market cap to trade at such elevated forward multiples for so long, with bulls and bears still deadlocked over unsupervised autonomy timelines.\n\nCathie Wood leaned into the autonomy and AI narrative, amplifying commentary on robotaxi share-shift flywheels versus traditional ride-hail and reiterating that token inference growth\u2014described as multiplying many-fold in a year\u2014remains poorly grasped across clients and companies even as frontier AI revenue scales.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "name": "Ives",
          "handle": "DivesTech",
